declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-101';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 22, $j$6.2 Prophecies of the Messiah$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Isaiah 7 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Isaiah/7"}, {"title": "Isaiah 53 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Isaiah/53"}, {"title": "Micah 5 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Micah/5"}, {"title": "Psalm 22 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Psalms/22"}, {"title": "Psalm 110 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Psalms/110"}], "body": "Read Isaiah 7:10 to 14, Isaiah 53, Micah 5:1 to 5, Psalm 22 and Psalm 110 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nThe word Messiah means anointed one, and the Greek form is Christ. The Old Testament contains many promises about this coming Savior, spoken hundreds of years before His birth. A virgin would conceive and bear a son named Immanuel. He would be born in Bethlehem. He would enter Jerusalem as a lowly King on a donkey. He would suffer for the sins of others, as Isaiah 53 describes, and Psalm 22 describes His suffering on the cross. Psalm 110 shows Him seated at the right hand of God. The New Testament shows that Jesus Christ fulfilled each of these.\n\nKey words: Messiah, Immanuel, suffering servant, fulfillment, anointed.\n\nYour task: (1) Name the town where Micah 5:2 says the ruler would be born. (2) Say what Immanuel means. (3) Tell two things Isaiah 53 says about the servant who suffered.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Last time we saw that the Old Testament is one story of promise. Today we look at specific prophecies about the coming Savior. The word Messiah means the anointed one. In Greek, that word is Christ. The prophets spoke of Him long before He came.\n\nLook at what Isaiah wrote around seven hundred years before Christ. Isaiah 7:14 says, Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel. When the New Testament quotes this verse in Matthew 1:23, it adds, which being interpreted is, God with us. The Savior would not be only a man sent from God. God Himself would come to be with us. First Timothy 3:16 says it plainly, God was manifest in the flesh.\n\nNext, the place of His birth. Micah wrote about seven hundred years before Christ that the ruler of Israel would come from Bethlehem. Micah 5:2 says, But thou, Bethlehem Ephratah, though thou be little among the thousands of Judah, yet out of thee shall he come forth unto me that is to be ruler in Israel; whose goings forth have been from of old, from everlasting. And Zechariah 9:9 told that the King would arrive in Jerusalem lowly, riding upon an ass.\n\nThe most striking prophecy is Isaiah 53, which describes a servant who would suffer for the sins of others. Isaiah 53:5 says, But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed. And verse 6 says, All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all. Read the chapter slowly and think about the cross.\n\nPsalm 22 also describes the suffering of the Messiah. David wrote about a thousand years before Christ, and he began, My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me? Verse 16 says, they pierced my hands and my feet. David wrote those words long before the Romans ever used crucifixion.\n\nFinally, Psalm 110:1 says, The LORD said unto my Lord, Sit thou at my right hand, until I make thine enemies thy footstool. The Messiah was not only to suffer. He was to reign.\n\nYour task is to name the town Micah mentions, tell what Immanuel means, and give two things Isaiah 53 says about the suffering servant. Then take the four question check.\n\nThe Savior was promised, and the Savior came. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.2 Prophecies of the Messiah$j$);
end
$seed$;
